WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:03.560
Have you

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.560
ever seen high school sports like this?

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.800
It's a pain endurance game.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.280
You hop on your knuckles.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.080
Ready? Hop!

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.560
It's done on closed fist.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.440
And so as you hop over the hardwood
floor, you get bloody knuckles.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.720
This is the seal hop.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.480
It's one of a series of gruelling
physical and mental challenges

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.480
that make up the NYO Games.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.000
Anchorage, Alaska.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.880
A modern city home to a fusion of cultures.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.680
Where the high life meets
the wildlife, and pride in the past

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
inspires the present and the future.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.280
And home to the NYO Games.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.640
Bringing together athletes

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.480
from all over the state
for three days of intense competition.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.400
But this is no ordinary high school
sports tournament.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.160
They're more than just games.
They're about survival.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.000
Athletes compete in a series of events

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.760
each of which has its own backstory
based on real life survival skills.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:13.280
We'll be finding out all
about the surprising origins of the games

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.000
and hearing
from some of the athletes taking part.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.400
But first, let's find out how the
NYO Games got started.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.120
And who better to tell us that
than Kyle Worl?

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.240
He grew up around the games.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.920
My dad was an athlete in the 80s and 90s.
Now he's an athlete himself

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.200
as well as a coach and mentor
to the next generation.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.400
But it's okay. We've practiced.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.320
These games have always been played
in our communities for hundreds of years.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.600
They were played during the winter months.
It was too dark or cold or

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.440
the weather was too severe to go outside.
And so the games were a way to pass time.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:02.200
And we brought it into the modern age.
The first NYO Games in 1971 saw 12 schools

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.000
Participate.
And now? There are over 50 teams

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.760
and almost 475 athletes.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.480
This is head official Nicole Johnson.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.800
She's been attending the games
for more than 40 years.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.000
My first NYO was in 1982
as a seventh grader. From high school

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.680
I started coaching.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.360
From coaching
I moved into an official position.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.480
And in 2004

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.560
I became NYO Games Alaska's head official.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.400
Nearly every single tribe
in the state of Alaska sends a team.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.280
And there are, I believe, 270

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.640
different tribes
within the state of Alaska.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.440
Today, the games are a way of sharing
and celebrating native traditions.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.200
Long ago, and still today, our people

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.280
live off the land.
They hunt, they fish, they gather.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.080
And it's not always an easy lifestyle.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.640
And so the games were intentionally made
to challenge you physically and mentally

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:07.320
so you that when you were faced
with situations - whether it was out

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.320
in a snowstorm or even enduring pain -

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.400
these games were a way to prepare you
for those situations.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.040
Every event at the NYO Games has a story.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.600
Here are just a few of them.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.800
The Intuit Stick Pull is a contest
of sheer strength and determination.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.160
Contestants try to pull their

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:31.000
opponent up off the floor,
or pull the stick away from their opponent,

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.280
without jerking, twisting,
or adjusting their grip.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.080
So when you come across a seal,
you harpoon the seal,

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:39.560
and then you pull out that harpoon.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:44.400
And so the Inuit Stick Pull represents
that motion of pulling in a seal.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.800
They can be 700 pounds or more.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.040
And so that's a tough battle.
If you let go, that's your food, right?

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:52.600
Your family goes hungry.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.480
So you really unlock
another level of strength.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.080
The One-Foot High Kick demands
strength, flexibility and balance.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.920
From a standing or running start,
athletes jump with both feet,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.400
kick a suspended ball with one foot,
then land on the kicking foot.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.160
Out on the tundra it's really flat.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.200
And on a clear day

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.920
you could see someone on the horizon.
And a high kick was used as sign language

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.200
back before we had radios or cell phones.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.200
And it was a way to signal either
A successful or unsuccessful hunt.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.240
And finally we have the Seal Hop.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.880
The most physically and mentally demanding
of all the events,

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.600
athletes must maintain a lowered
push up position with elbows bent,

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.880
hands curled and knuckles down.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.360
At the official’s signal

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.480
contestants hop across the floor,
mimicking the movement of a seal.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.880
The reason they would do this is
because as hunters,

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.200
we had to figure out how to get close
enough to the seal without scaring it off.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.520
That was their trick
to get close enough to the seal, jump up

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.520
with the harpoon, and they'd have a seal
to bring home to your community.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.160
And it's a very challenging one.
You get bloody hands from it.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.080
Now we've learned about the games

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.240
it's time to meet some of the student
athletes taking part.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.400
My name is Don Heflin.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.320
Inupiaq
from my mom's side, from Nome, Alaska.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.000
My name is Leif Richards.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.400
I live in Juneau

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.520
but my family's from the village of Igiugig.
My name's Mila Neely.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:34.160
I'm a citizen of the Cherokee Nation and
I was born and raised in Juneau, Alaska.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.640
I compete in a couple of different events.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.760
I compete in the One-Foot High Kick,
Two-Foot High Kick,

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:41.040
Scissor Broad Jump,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.040
Toe Kick, Kneel Jump and Seal Hop.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.800
I compete in all the games
but this year at State I competed

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:51.520
in the One-Foot High Kick, the One-Arm Reach and Scissor Broad.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.720
I compete in a lot of different events.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.560
I do all three kicking events:

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:56.320
One-Foot, Two-Foot,

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.200
Alaskan, I do Wrist Carry,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.960
Dene, Stick Pull.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.400
What am I forgetting? Oh, Seal Hop!

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.520
That's one of my favorites.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.280
I like Seal Hope because it's like an
endurance game and it's at the very end.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.440
It's just, like, all the leftover energy
you have, just like all out.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.040
I think if my ancestors and family
were here seeing me do these games

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.200
I think they would be really proud of me
because that means I'm still

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.520
celebrating our culture.
I do feel connected to the history.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.720
All the games are about survival
and helping each other out. Doing the games

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:28.800
makes me feel very connected
to the community and my culture.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.360
It really connects me even though I'm not
Alaska Native, I'm Cherokee.

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:35.360
It kind of helps
connect me to my indigenous roots.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.480
I think if
someone from a different part of the world

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:49.040
saw us doing NYO of it would kind of, like,
inspire them.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.040
Our people had to be very resilient,
Strong, tough people.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:59.320
And these games were all created
to help develop

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:05.240
those skills that they needed to survive.
Joining NYO was really life changing

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:11.560
because it was a space where I didn't
have to hide my indigenous identity.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:15.200
I was able to
really build my own confidence and connect

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:18.200
with my native identity
in a positive way.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.480
I feel a greater sense
of belonging in my community,

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:22.800
A sense of purpose.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.120
And I have these games to thank.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:29.640
You good?

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:32.240
I’m proud of you.

